WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Can a light microscope separate two proteins 50 nm apart?

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.280
Through a standard lens, each glowing label spreads into a wide blur.

3
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.440
On a vesicle, both blurs merge into one smudge.

4
00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:21.007
Each glowing point forms an Airy pattern, a bright center with dark rings.

5
00:00:22.207 --> 00:00:26.227
Two spots resolve when one center hits the other's first dark ring.

6
00:00:27.507 --> 00:00:32.647
Emission wavelength and numerical aperture, the lens's light cone, set that Rayleigh

7
00:00:32.647 --> 00:00:42.207
distance. Using 520 nm green light, numerical aperture 1.00 gives 317.2 nm.

8
00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:49.733
Losing 0.4 in numerical aperture costs about 90 nm of resolution.

9
00:00:51.313 --> 00:00:57.273
Predict: two GFP-tagged proteins sit 50 nm apart on a synaptic vesicle.

10
00:00:58.553 --> 00:01:03.393
With numerical aperture 1.4, can a standard microscope separate them?

11
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:16.840
No. 317.2 minus about 90 leaves about 230 nm.

12
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.420
That limit is more than four times 50.

13
00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.260
Zooming only enlarges that blur: magnification without resolution is empty

14
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:33.640
magnification. Super-resolution changes the measurement, controlling when and where

15
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.500
molecules glow so overlapping blurs separate.

16
00:01:37.683 --> 00:01:43.843
In 2002, George Patterson built photoactivatable GFP in Jennifer

17
00:01:43.843 --> 00:01:45.163
Lippincott-Schwartz's lab.

18
00:01:46.503 --> 00:01:48.983
It starts dark; violet light switches it on.

19
00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:56.703
In 2006, Eric Betzig and Harald Hess published PALM, alongside Xiaowei Zhuang's

20
00:01:56.703 --> 00:01:58.723
STORM and Samuel Hess's FPALM.

21
00:01:59.943 --> 00:02:03.303
Switch on a sparse few molecules so blurs never overlap.

22
00:02:04.403 --> 00:02:07.963
Locate each center, then let them bleach, losing their glow.

23
00:02:09.323 --> 00:02:10.603
Repeat thousands of times.

24
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:15.530
One lone blur can be centered far more sharply than its width.

25
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:22.410
In the shot-noise approximation, uncertainty equals blur standard deviation over the

26
00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.490
square root of photons.

27
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:28.550
Take a 100 nm blur giving 100 photons.

28
00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:35.050
The square root of 100 is 10; 100 ÷ 10 gives about 10 nm.

29
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:39.350
Switching sparse molecules is not the only route.

30
00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:45.630
Stefan Hell proposed STED in 1994 and showed it in 1999.

31
00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:52.030
A ring beam switches off fluorescence around the rim, leaving a tiny glowing center.

32
00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:57.690
Structured illumination instead computes finer detail from striped lighting.

33
00:02:58.733 --> 00:03:04.413
That shot-noise formula neglects background and pixel size, and crowded molecules

34
00:03:04.413 --> 00:03:05.553
blur together again.

35
00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:10.773
Once a camera samples finely enough, further zoom adds no detail.

36
00:03:12.133 --> 00:03:17.233
Comparing live, fixed, and primary cells tests whether a pattern holds, without

37
00:03:17.233 --> 00:03:18.893
claiming every cell shares it.

38
00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:23.647
PALM, STORM and FPALM reach tens-of-nanometers detail.

39
00:03:25.247 --> 00:03:31.407
Stone and colleagues imaged CH27 mouse B-cell lymphoma cells, live and fixed; fixed

40
00:03:31.407 --> 00:03:33.127
primary mouse B cells agreed.

41
00:03:34.427 --> 00:03:39.067
A peptide probe preferring ordered membrane gathered near clustered B-cell

42
00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:42.587
receptors; a disordered-membrane probe was excluded.

43
00:03:43.807 --> 00:03:47.747
That supports phase-like sorting in that system, not in every cell.

44
00:03:48.787 --> 00:03:53.107
Controlling which molecules glow turns overlapping blurs into maps.
